# Copy to .env and fill in. The daemon refuses to start without a credential.
#   REEMOAT_TOKEN=$(openssl rand -hex 16)
REEMOAT_TOKEN=

# Which file the supervisor sources, and the one variable that cannot live in it.
#
# Nothing in this repository reads a `.env` on its own — there is no dotenv
# dependency and no `--env-file` in `src/` or `scripts/`. `deploy/run-daemon.sh`
# is what `.`-sources a file, and REEMOAT_ENV_FILE is how it is told which one
# (default: ~/.reemoat/daemon.env). Setting it *here* is circular, so it belongs
# in the supervisor's own environment — and a host installed with an override
# needs it in `deploy/deploy.sh`'s environment too, which is the same fact stated
# in .claude/rules/deployment.md.

# How the daemon decides who is asking.
#   shared_secret (default) — one secret, all scopes, no expiry, no control
#                             plane anywhere. The single-machine case, and the
#                             zero-configuration way to try this: `pnpm client`
#                             and `pnpm harness` both work against it with nothing
#                             else set up.
#   signed                  — only tokens signed by the control plane this
#                             machine enrolled with. REEMOAT_TOKEN is ignored.
#   both                    — accepts either, chosen by shape.
#
# `both` is a migration and break-glass mode, not a destination: the shared
# secret bypasses every grant and every scope the control plane issued. It
# exists because enrolling a daemon you reach over the network is exactly the
# moment you can lock yourself out of it.
# REEMOAT_AUTH=shared_secret

# Where to enroll, and the single-use code to do it with. Needed once, ever.
# The daemon exchanges the code for its machine id and the public keys that
# verify tokens addressed to it, writes both to REEMOAT_DB, and never contacts
# the control plane again — not to refresh a key, not to check a revocation
# list. That is why a control-plane outage cannot touch a running session, and
# why a daemon with no control plane at all still works.
#
# Nothing the daemon caches expires. Only the *user's* token does, and the
# client renews that, not the daemon.
#
# Restarting with the same code makes no network call; restarting with a new one
# re-enrolls, which is also how a control-plane signing key is rotated. Codes
# are single-use and expire after an hour.
# REEMOAT_CONTROL_PLANE=https://control-plane.example
# REEMOAT_ENROLL_CODE=ec_…

# **Every client reaches this daemon through the control plane's relay, and there
# is no other way in.** A daemon that was handed a relay at enrollment dials out
# over WSS and holds one connection; browsers and `pnpm client` reach it through
# that. There is nothing to switch on, and `REEMOAT_RELAY=0` is gone: opting out
# of the relay is opting out of being reachable, which is not a configuration.
#
# The cost, stated where it will be read: this daemon holds a permanent outbound
# connection to its control plane, and an outage there costs *all* reachability.
# What it does not cost is anything running — verification is entirely local, the
# tunnel is never *asked* anything, and a session in flight neither notices nor
# stops. That is the half the whole design rests on and it is untouched.
#
# What it buys, and what a plain tunnel cannot: the relay checks the caller's
# signed token **and their grant** before a byte enters the tunnel, against live
# rows. So revoking a grant takes effect on the next request rather than within a
# token lifetime.
#
# REEMOAT_RELAY is still *read*, and for one reason: to warn when it is set. A
# value left behind in an env file after the switch disappeared would otherwise
# look like it was still deciding something.
# REEMOAT_RELAY=   # deprecated, ignored

# Where to bind. Loopback, and this is the line that makes the paragraph above
# true rather than merely intended.
#
# It used to be 0.0.0.0 so a Tailnet address worked without extra configuration,
# back when a browser reached a daemon directly. Nothing outside addresses this
# listener now: the relay routes by the verified `aud` claim and splices each
# CONNECT to a fresh loopback connection here.
#
# Clearing a machine's address in the registry was never the lever on its own —
# the daemon went on listening on every interface regardless, and a client that
# had already memoised a direct route went on using it. This is.
#
# The port stays known and stays 7887, because the things that *do* address it
# are on this machine: `pnpm client` under the shared secret, and the deploy
# script's /health probe. REEMOAT_PORT=0 works for a daemon that is only ever
# served by the relay.
REEMOAT_HOST=127.0.0.1
REEMOAT_PORT=7887

# ⚠ **One client is allowed to address this listener now, and it is the one
# running on this computer.** The Reemoat desktop app reaches a daemon on the same
# machine over loopback instead of out to the relay and back — never a LAN address
# and never a hostname, which is why the line above still says what it says.
#
# It finds it because an enrolled daemon writes daemon.json into its state
# directory (REEMOAT_HOME below, ~/.reemoat by default) when it starts: the
# machine id, the loopback address and the port it actually bound, at
# 0600 inside a 0700 directory. That ownership is the point rather than a
# precaution — the app has to show a machine token to prove anything to a daemon,
# and a file another OS user could write is a port they could point at themselves.
# There is nothing to configure: a daemon on a custom REEMOAT_PORT, or on 0, is
# found the same way, and a `shared_secret` daemon writes nothing at all because it
# has no identity to announce. The file is removed on a clean stop.
#
# The app switches it off per machine, in Settings, and says what it costs there:
# the relay is what reads live grant rows before each request, so on this one path
# a revoked grant keeps working for up to the token's ~360s. Everywhere else it
# stops at once. docs/DECISIONS.md Q7.137.

# Where the directory picker starts. PATH-style, like the shell's.
#
# **A narrowing of the listing, and not a boundary.** The agent runs as the user
# this daemon runs as, so it can already reach anything that user can — a narrower
# root is about not scrolling past `/Library` on a phone, not about safety. `cwd`
# is deliberately *not* confined to it, so a repository you keep in /opt or on an
# external volume is still somewhere a session can start.
#
# Unset, the picker starts at this user's home.
# REEMOAT_ROOTS=

# REEMOAT_USER_ROOT is deprecated and gets its own warning at startup rather than
# being ignored quietly, because its value names a directory that holds real
# sessions and worktrees written by a daemon that no longer reads there. Agents
# run as this user, on this filesystem; there is no per-user tree any more, and
# REEMOAT_ROOTS above is what chooses where browsing starts.
# REEMOAT_USER_ROOT=   # deprecated, ignored

# The container settings are deprecated too, and warned about together for the
# same reason: agents no longer run in containers at all, so a leftover memory
# or cpu bound is not a bound that got smaller — it is one that stopped existing.
#   REEMOAT_CONTAINER_IMAGE, REEMOAT_CONTAINER_HOME, REEMOAT_CONTAINER_USER,
#   REEMOAT_CONTAINER_MEMORY, REEMOAT_CONTAINER_CPUS, REEMOAT_CONTAINER_PIDS,
#   REEMOAT_MAX_CONTAINERS

# The directory every default below is derived from: the database, the worktrees,
# the uploads, the plugins, the ask directory and the local announcement
# (daemon.json). An explicit REEMOAT_DB or REEMOAT_*_ROOT still wins over it. It is
# not where the agent CLIs live: ~/.reemoat/toolchain is per user and shared by
# every daemon this account runs, and so are the agents' own sign-ins.
#
# ⚠ Set by the desktop app for each daemon it starts — ~/.reemoat for the server
# ~/.reemoat/daemon.env names, ~/.reemoat/servers/<server>/ for every other, so
# one computer on two servers is two databases rather than one identity enrolled
# back and forth. Leave it unset in a file a service sources: the app looks only
# at ~/.reemoat/daemon.json and ~/.reemoat/servers/<server>/daemon.json, so a
# launchd or systemd daemon moved anywhere else is one it stops seeing — and it
# would then start a second daemon of its own on ~/.reemoat. It also does not
# carry REEMOAT_AGENT_UPDATES, REEMOAT_AGENT_SOURCE or REEMOAT_AGENT_CHANNEL from
# ~/.reemoat/daemon.env to those other daemons; each reads only its own file.
# docs/DECISIONS.md Q7.148.
# Default: ~/.reemoat
# REEMOAT_HOME=

# Where durable state lives: the event log and session metadata, in one SQLite
# file. Survives a restart — a session that was running when the daemon died
# comes back as `interrupted` with its transcript and sequence numbers intact,
# and can be resumed. The file holds full agent transcripts and every file diff,
# so it is created 0600 inside a 0700 directory.
# Default: $REEMOAT_HOME/reemoat.db (~/.reemoat/reemoat.db)   Use :memory: for the
# old, non-durable behaviour.
# REEMOAT_DB=

# What the startup prune may delete, and when it may delete anything at all.
#
# A session is pruned only when it is *inactive* — ended by you (Stop, or
# signing the agent out), by the agent, never started at all, or given up on
# because the agent said it no longer holds the conversation (the daemon stops
# trying by itself; a manual Resume still can, until the prune takes the row)
# — and untouched for REEMOAT_SESSION_TTL_DAYS, counted from its last write
# rather than from when it was opened. A live session, and one the daemon
# itself ended on a restart or a deploy and is still coming back to, is never
# pruned, at any age and under any cap: that is the daemon's own promise to put
# an agent back on it, and a machine put down for a week comes back holding
# its conversations. Above REEMOAT_MAX_SESSIONS inactive rows the least
# recently touched go too, pins last, so the table may exceed the cap by
# exactly the rows it never prunes. And a prune never leaves fewer than
# REEMOAT_MIN_SESSIONS rows — the ones it never prunes first, then pins, then
# the most recently touched, whatever their age — so nothing at all is deleted
# while the table holds fewer than that; a list that short is scrolled and
# pruned by hand. Set it to 1 and the floor protects one row — an active one
# whenever there is any, which it keeps regardless, so while a session is live
# the floor adds nothing (0 reads as unset); set above the cap it leaves the
# cap nothing to cut rather than letting it cut under the floor. Measured
# 2026-09-04: the rule before this one keyed on creation, and one restart
# deleted five of six live conversations with their transcripts and no line in
# the journal. What is pruned is printed at startup now, with every id, and
# disappears from GET /sessions.
# REEMOAT_SESSION_TTL_DAYS=7
# REEMOAT_MAX_SESSIONS=200
# REEMOAT_MIN_SESSIONS=50

# On startup, SIGKILL agent process groups the previous daemon left behind. Only
# ever attempted for sessions created since the last boot — pids are recycled and
# a reboot resets them. Set to 0 to leave orphans alone.
# REEMOAT_REAP_ORPHANS=1

# On startup, put a fresh agent back on every session this daemon itself ended —
# a restart, a deploy, a crash — so a conversation survives being redeployed and
# you go on talking to it in the same context. A session you stopped by hand is
# never resumed; that is the one thing "stopped" means. The same switch also
# governs the transparent resume when you send a message to an interrupted
# session, because an operator who turned this off to stop agents spawning at
# boot did not mean "spawn them on a keystroke instead".
#
# The interrupted *turn* is not re-run. The agent comes back with the whole
# conversation and idle, not carrying on where it was cut off.
# REEMOAT_AUTO_RESUME=1

# Whether an agent may stop and ask you a question instead of guessing.
#
# This withdraws a *tool*, not a piece of UI. Measured against claude-agent-acp
# 0.63.0: the adapter puts "AskUserQuestion" into disallowedTools unless the
# client declares clientCapabilities.elicitation.form, so with this off the model
# never sees the tool and cannot ask at all. It also governs questions forwarded
# from MCP servers and the CLI's model-fallback consent prompt. The 0.73.0 pin
# carries the same expression, character for character.
#
# codex-acp 1.1.9 reads the same capability with the same expression and answers
# its own ask-the-user tool with empty answers when it is absent, so this switch
# means the same thing there. Note codex keeps that tool behind its own
# "default_mode_request_user_input" feature flag, which ships off — so turning
# this on is necessary for a codex question and not sufficient. (That flag name is
# no longer in codex-acp 1.8.0's bundle, and no codex question was drawn live on
# 2026-09-04, so the 1.8.0 shape is unmeasured rather than confirmed.)
#
# Why you might turn it off: a question parks the turn on a human until somebody
# answers or the turn is swept. On a machine nobody is watching — a scheduled
# session, a driver — that turns "finishes" into "waits". Same reasoning as the
# switch above, and the same shape of regret if you get it wrong.
# REEMOAT_ELICITATION=1

# Whether a claude session nobody has decided about asks for ultracode: highest
# effort, and every turn planned as a workflow of subagents. It is claude's own
# session setting, not an ACP option, so it is asked for at the door — which is why
# changing it on a live session restarts the agent (the conversation is kept, the
# same way a deploy keeps it) and why this switch is what makes that restart rare.
#
# Per session it is a row in the effort menu, and a choice made there outranks this
# in both directions and for ever. Only claude has it; kimi and codex ignore it. It
# also needs a model that supports the highest effort level — the row is absent on
# one that does not, which is the agent's own answer rather than a list of ours.
# REEMOAT_CLAUDE_ULTRACODE=1

# Which build of an agent's CLI this daemon runs. Both are the *vendor's* own
# variables rather than ours, and each is read by the adapter that spawns that
# CLI — `claude-agent-acp` reads the first thing in `claudeCliPath()`, and
# `codex-acp` reads the second in `startAcpServer()`. The daemon writes whichever
# copy it chose into that variable on every spawn, so it is set for every session
# whether or not you set it here; setting it here is how you *choose*.
#
# With neither set, the daemon runs the first copy it finds: on PATH in order, then
# in the directories `deploy/agents.sh` installs into (`~/.local/bin`,
# `~/.opencode/bin`, `~/.reemoat/toolchain/bin`). So a copy of your own on the
# service's PATH outranks the one this daemon keeps current, and a machine with
# neither has no harness at all — nothing is vendored under `node_modules` any more,
# and the tile says so rather than failing at spawn. Which build published the model
# list on screen is reported beside it, off the same spawn.
#
# What setting one costs: this is daemon-wide, not per session, and it retargets the
# *login* as well — `LocalRuntime.agentCli` reads the same variable first, for the
# login and the session alike, which is what keeps a credential from being written
# for a build no session runs. It is also left alone by the daily refresh below: an
# operator who named a file meant it. Point it at a binary that cannot start and
# every session on that harness fails.
# CLAUDE_CODE_EXECUTABLE=/path/to/claude
# CODEX_PATH=/path/to/codex
#
# ⚠ CODEX_HOME is NOT the same thing and must not be set for this. `CODEX_PATH`
# names the binary; `CODEX_HOME` names where codex keeps its credentials, and
# only the first is what `AGENT_LOGIN.codex.executableEnv` reads. Both survive
# `agentEnv()`'s strip, which is deliberate: an operator who set one meant it.
#
# kimi and opencode have no such variable, and they do not need one: each *is* the
# program, so the daemon runs the file it found rather than telling an adapter
# about it.

# Whether this daemon keeps the agent CLIs current by itself. On by default, and
# that is the decision rather than an oversight: not one of the four self-updates
# when a daemon drives it over ACP — every one of their updaters is gated on a
# terminal a spawned agent never has — so left alone a machine publishes the model
# list of whatever shipped with the release, with no error and nothing on screen.
# Measured 2026-09-03 on the machine this was written on: kimi 0.29.2 against 0.40.1
# upstream and codex 0.146.1 against 0.153.0, both months behind and both never run
# in a terminal there, while claude — which *is* — was current.
#
# What it does: five minutes after start, then daily with jitter, it runs
# `deploy/agents.sh --refresh-only` — which moves the copies this machine already
# has, through the door each came in by, and fetches **nothing new**. No daemon
# restart, so no turn in flight is interrupted; a build a live session may still be
# on is kept until a run finds none. This is NOT the daemon updating itself — see
# src/version.ts, which still holds. Q4.113.
#
# What it does NOT do, since 0.9.x: install a harness that is not here. Nothing puts
# a coding-agent CLI on a machine but somebody pressing Install in the app — which is
# what stops a harness added to Reemoat arriving on every machine in a fleet by
# itself, offering a sign-in for a program nobody asked for. `deploy/bootstrap.sh
# --install-agents claude,codex` is the door for a provisioner with nobody to press
# anything. Q4.126.
#
# Turn it off for a machine with no outbound network, or one whose CLIs somebody else
# manages: `off`, `0`, `false`, `no` and `never` all mean off. That switches off the
# **button** as well as the timer — a machine whose CLIs are managed elsewhere does
# not want a control that downloads one either, and the app draws none. A harness
# whose binary is named in CLAUDE_CODE_EXECUTABLE or CODEX_PATH above is left alone
# by the script, because an override wins outright and a copy refreshed beside it
# would never run.
# REEMOAT_AGENT_UPDATES=off

# Where that script gets the CLIs from. `vendor` (the default) is each vendor's own
# installer, which is what `deploy/agents.sh` documents; `npm` is the same
# programs — `@anthropic-ai/claude-code`, `@openai/codex`, `opencode-ai`,
# `@moonshot-ai/kimi-code`, `@xai-official/grok` — from the npm registry instead,
# into `~/.reemoat/toolchain`, for a machine that cannot reach claude.ai,
# chatgpt.com or opencode.ai. Two of the five take that door under either value:
# kimi, because its own updater exits 0 without installing when there is no TTY,
# and grok, because its vendor installer edits shell profiles and this script
# edits none. Point npm at your mirror the way npm is pointed anywhere
# (`npm_config_registry`, or `~/.npmrc`); this daemon reads neither. It is a choice
# rather than a fallback: a vendor outage does not quietly switch a machine to a
# differently built binary. The one-line installer's `--agent-source npm` writes this
# line and passes the same value to its own first run, so the two never disagree.
# It decides how a CLI that is missing is installed; a CLI already on the machine
# keeps being refreshed the way it was installed, whatever this says — and under
# `npm` a vendor-installed copy, which the registry cannot refresh, is reported as
# a warning on every daily run until it is removed, after which the next run
# installs it from the registry. Anything but `vendor` or `npm` is reported at
# start and read as `vendor`.
# REEMOAT_AGENT_SOURCE=vendor

# Which of claude's two release channels this machine follows. `latest` (the
# default) is the newest build Anthropic has published; `stable` trails it by
# weeks, and the model list is whatever the binary publishes, so a machine on
# `stable` is missing the newest model with no error. Measured 2026-09-05: `stable`
# answered 2.1.236 and `latest` 2.1.261, and only the second knows
# `claude-fable-5-1`. It applies to claude's vendor installer only — codex's and
# opencode's have no channel, and under `npm` all five are `@latest`. The script
# hands it to `claude install <channel>` on every run rather than running `claude
# update`, which follows whichever channel the last install wrote into
# ~/.claude/settings.json — so changing this moves the machine to that channel's
# version on the next run, down as well as up, keeping the old build on disk and
# interrupting no session. The one-line installer's `--agent-channel stable`
# writes this line. Anything but `stable` or `latest` is reported at start and
# read as `latest`.
# REEMOAT_AGENT_CHANNEL=latest

# Each session gets its own git worktree, so two agents on one repo do not fight
# over one index and one HEAD — which is most of what makes running several at
# once bearable. A worktree branches from a commit, so uncommitted work in the
# main checkout is NOT in it; the session's log carries a warning saying so.
# Worktrees are never removed automatically.
#   auto (default) — make one whenever the cwd is a git repo with a commit
#   require        — refuse to create a session that cannot have one
#   never          — off daemon-wide
# REEMOAT_WORKTREE=auto
# Where they live. Somewhere out of the way, which is the whole requirement:
#   $REEMOAT_HOME/worktrees/<repo>/<session-id>   (~/.reemoat/worktrees/… by default)
# REEMOAT_WORKTREE_ROOT=

# Files attached to a prompt from the web UI, staged here before the agent is
# told about them:
#   $REEMOAT_HOME/uploads/<session-id>/<upload-id>/<name>   (~/.reemoat/uploads/… by default)
# Beside the worktrees rather than inside one, on purpose: a worktree can be
# removed with `DELETE /sessions/:id/workspace` while staged input has to outlive
# that, and for a session running in a plain directory the workspace root IS your
# own checkout — attachments would land in your repository and show up as
# untracked files. The daemon refuses to start if this and REEMOAT_WORKTREE_ROOT
# contain one another, because each of the two `rm` paths is guarded by a
# containment check against its own root and nesting them makes both meaningless.
# REEMOAT_UPLOAD_ROOT=
# Where installed plugins live, one directory per plugin per version. Default
#   $REEMOAT_HOME/plugins/<plugin-id>/<version>/   (~/.reemoat/plugins/… by default)
# The **third** remover tree, and the daemon refuses to start if any two of the
# three contain one another — this, REEMOAT_UPLOAD_ROOT and REEMOAT_WORKTREE_ROOT
# — for the reason the paragraph above gives: each `rm` path is guarded by a
# containment check against its own root, and nesting them makes all of them
# meaningless.
# REEMOAT_PLUGIN_ROOT=
# Plugins off entirely. A plugin is somebody else's code running on this machine
# as you, so an operator who does not want that here should not have to uninstall
# anything to say so. With this at 0 the plugin routes answer 503 rather than
# reporting an empty list, because "there are none" and "this daemon does not do
# that" are different answers. See docs/PLUGINS.md.
# REEMOAT_PLUGINS=1
# Branch names are <prefix>/<session-id>, so `git branch --list "reemoat/*"` is
# the whole clean-up story.
# REEMOAT_BRANCH_PREFIX=reemoat

# Changes-API bounds. Tunable for the same reason as the log bounds below: so the
# truncation path can be exercised without manufacturing 2000 changed files.
# REEMOAT_CHANGES_MAX_FILES=2000
# REEMOAT_DIFF_MAX_BYTES=524288

# Per-session event log bounds. Optional, and there is NO DEFAULT: a session's
# log is never truncated.
#
# It used to default to 5000 events / 8 MiB, evicting a *prefix* — which meant a
# conversation you were still working in could lose its beginning, permanently,
# with the client unable to tell that from a conversation that started there.
# Measured on a real session: `dropped: 6144`, oldest surviving event the two
# characters " for". There is no number that makes that acceptable, because the
# part a prefix eviction takes first is the part that says what the work is.
#
# Setting these turns eviction back on for this daemon, which is worth having for
# a machine with a small disk and for exercising the path — but understand that
# it destroys the oldest part of long conversations.
#
# They no longer need to stay below the WS outbound queue bound: `attach` caps its
# own replay (ATTACH_REPLAY_MAX in src/server.ts) and tells the client to page the
# rest over HTTP, so an unbounded log no longer collapses a `since=0` attach.
#
# What still bounds the database is whole sessions rather than parts of one —
# inactive sessions untouched for 7 days, and the 200 cap on inactive rows,
# pruned at startup and only past 50 rows, since the floor keeps fifty whatever
# their age; see REEMOAT_MIN_SESSIONS above.
# REEMOAT_LOG_EVENTS=5000
# REEMOAT_LOG_BYTES=8388608

# How many sessions may run at once here, and how fast new ones may be made.
#
# Backstops rather than opinions, and they exist because `POST /sessions` had no
# bound of any kind: it ran a real `git worktree add` and spawned an agent, once
# per request, for ever. The only thing that counted sessions was the startup
# prune — and that counts in order to *delete*: it kept the newest 200 rows by
# creation and took every other transcript with it. (It cuts only inactive rows
# now — never a live one, nor one the daemon is coming back to — and keeps at
# least 50, those first, then pins, then the most recently touched; but it is
# still a deletion.)
#
# That was harmless while a daemon served one person. A grant is what makes it
# matter: anybody you share a machine with can create sessions on it, and a loop
# fills the table past the cap with empty rows, so the next restart deletes your
# conversations. The daemon deliberately does not know *who* is asking, so the
# bound is on the rate rather than on the person — which is the right shape
# anyway, since what makes the prune destructive is how fast rows appear.
#
# The live ceiling counts sessions that hold, or are entitled to, an agent —
# processes, not conversations. Stopping one frees a slot, and so does leaving one
# alone: see REEMOAT_IDLE_PARK_MINUTES below. Resuming never *refuses*, so a daemon
# coming back from a deploy always restores the work it was holding; a wake that
# would cross the ceiling parks the least recently used session instead.
#
# The burst is spent by any create that gets past the ceiling, including one that
# then fails — the alternative is doing the expensive part before deciding
# whether to refuse. Both are in memory, so a restart clears them.
# REEMOAT_MAX_LIVE_SESSIONS=64
# REEMOAT_SESSION_CREATE_BURST=16
# REEMOAT_SESSION_CREATE_REFILL_MS=120000

# How long a session may sit untouched before the daemon lets go of its agent.
#
# The process is killed; nothing else the daemon can keep is. The conversation, the
# worktree, the branch and the title all stay exactly where they are, the whole
# transcript stays readable, and the next message you send starts an agent again and
# carries on — over the same session/resume the daemon already performs after each of
# its own restarts. It is not "stopped": nobody stopped it, and the session says so,
# with its own state rather than the one DELETE /sessions/:id writes.
#
# The one exception is work the agent left running in the background, which dies with
# the process: the CLI kills what it backgrounded when it is shut down, and a resumed
# agent is told nothing about it. So a session claude says is still running something
# is never released until that work ends — which is why a machine can hold more agents
# than this number would suggest, and why a create can be refused while an agent sits
# there apparently idle. Only claude reports this, and not for backgrounded subagents;
# for the others the daemon still cannot tell, and the old behaviour stands.
#
# Both numbers behind the default were measured on one machine, 2026-09-09, and
# they are the whole argument. A live claude session is 259-341 MB of resident
# memory (an ACP bridge plus the CLI under it) and a live opencode session
# 447-476 MB, mean ~397 MB — and five of them held 1 984 MB, of which 1 384 MB
# belonged to three sessions nobody had touched in 48.7 hours. Coming back costs
# 1 292 ms at the median (claude, resuming alone, over 28 real reattaches) and
# 2 376 ms at p90. So roughly 400 MB is held for every second-and-a-third saved.
#
# Thirty minutes is past every pause that happens inside a working session while
# still releasing what is left overnight, which is where the waste actually is.
# Lower it if you would rather have the memory and do not mind meeting the wait;
# 0 switches it off entirely, including the eviction a wake would otherwise do.
#
# This is the one setting here with a control on a screen: the machine's own
# settings page can save a value, and a saved value overrides this variable and
# takes effect without a restart. That is deliberate rather than a leak in
# "the daemon's config is env only" — the number is one person's trade between
# memory on their own machine and a ~1.3s wait, so the person using the machine
# owns it. So this is the default for a machine nobody has set, and stops
# deciding once somebody has; the way back to it is typing the number.
# REEMOAT_IDLE_PARK_MINUTES=30

# How long a turn may go without one byte from the agent before the daemon stops
# waiting for it.
#
# This is not a limit on how long a turn may take. A turn that runs for six hours
# while the agent narrates what it is doing is untouched; what this bounds is
# *silence*, and only inside a turn.
#
# It exists because `session/prompt` is the one request the daemon sends with no
# deadline — deliberately, since a real turn may run for hours — and a session
# reads "running" for exactly as long as that request has not been answered.
# An adapter that stops answering therefore pins a session at "working" until the
# daemon restarts: Stop cannot clear it (it waits on the same answer), and the
# idle sweep cannot see it (it only looks at idle sessions). Reported as a panel
# still saying "working" hours after the agent had finished.
#
# When it fires, nothing is sent to the agent. The agent is not stopped, not
# cancelled and not told; the daemon simply stops claiming the turn is in flight,
# writes one line into that conversation saying the agent stopped answering, and
# lets you type again. If the agent does eventually say something, it still lands
# in the transcript. The session becomes idle, so REEMOAT_IDLE_PARK_MINUTES then
# applies to it normally and the stuck agent is released the ordinary way.
#
# A session waiting on *you* — a permission, a question — is never touched by
# this, however long it waits, and neither is one claude says is still running
# background work. Your own messages do not count as activity either: what is
# measured is how long the *agent* has been silent, because typing into a session
# that says "working" used to reset the clock and keep the stuck state alive.
#
# Three hours, and the number is measured rather than picked. It was an hour, on
# the reasoning that what goes quiet inside a turn is one long tool call — a build,
# a test suite — and that an hour is past all of those. Read off the reporting
# machine's own database, the turn that produced this bug report ran 88 minutes,
# ended with a real reply from the agent, and contained a single silence of 51.7
# minutes. An hour would have cleared it by eight minutes. So the threshold is a
# multiple of the one silence anybody has measured rather than a margin over it,
# and it errs large on purpose: ending a turn that was not over puts your next
# message in front of an agent still answering the last one and writes a line
# saying it stopped when it had not, while waiting too long is only the old
# behaviour, bounded instead of endless.
#
# Unlike the setting above, there is no screen for this one and no saved override:
# the honest value is a property of the agents rather than of the machine, and a
# number anybody can lower from a phone is a number that can cut live turns short.
# 0 switches it off; the session then stays "working" for ever, as it used to.
# REEMOAT_TURN_SILENCE_MINUTES=180


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by `pnpm client`, not by the daemon.
#
# They are here because this is the only file that documents an environment for
# this repository, and the alternative was what happened: two of them appeared in
# no document at all and the other two only in CLAUDE.md. A daemon that is handed
# any of these ignores every one of them, which is why they sit in a section of
# their own rather than beside the settings above.
#
# `pnpm client --help` prints all four with their live values, so the check for
# "did this get through" is that command rather than this file.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# One daemon on this machine, addressed directly, with the shared secret above.
# Default: http://127.0.0.1:7887 — the same port REEMOAT_PORT defaults to, since
# what addresses that listener is on this machine by construction.
#
# It is also a switch and not only an address: with REEMOAT_URL set explicitly the
# client will not re-resolve a route after a failure, because there is nothing to
# re-resolve and retrying only doubles the time it takes to report the same error.
# REEMOAT_URL=http://127.0.0.1:7887

# Or, against a control plane. **All three, or none** — the client is in
# control-plane mode only when every one of them is non-empty, and then it asks
# for a short-lived token for one machine, learns that machine's URL from the same
# answer, and renews before it expires, including across an expiry mid-stream.
# REEMOAT_TOKEN and REEMOAT_URL are unused in that mode.
#
# The key is one of the account's own API keys (`rk_…`, minted in the web UI or
# with `cpctl`), and it never leaves this machine: what reaches the daemon is the
# token the control plane signs for it. The machine is whatever you call it —
# your own label for it, its `m_…` id, or the row's real name, resolved in that
# order and never against somebody else's label.
# REEMOAT_CP_URL=https://control-plane.example
# REEMOAT_CP_KEY=rk_…
# REEMOAT_MACHINE=laptop

# REEMOAT_ROUTE is deprecated and warned about, along with the choice it pinned:
# it selected between a direct URL and the relay, which was a real decision while
# both existed. Against a control plane there is one path now — the tunnel the
# daemon dialled out — and without one there is REEMOAT_URL on loopback.
# REEMOAT_ROUTE=   # deprecated, ignored
